# DPDK for Raspberry Pi 5 RP1 Ethernet

Experimental DPDK support for the **Raspberry Pi 5 RP1 Ethernet controller**, based on the Cadence MACB/GEM Ethernet MAC.

This repository adds a custom DPDK Poll Mode Driver (PMD) that allows the Raspberry Pi 5's onboard Ethernet controller to be accessed directly from userspace using DPDK.

> **Status:** Research prototype / work in progress.

## Overview

The RP1 Ethernet controller is normally managed by the Linux `macb` driver.

This project instead:

```text
RP1 Ethernet
    ↓
uio_pdrv_genirq
    ↓
/dev/uio0
    ↓
DPDK MACB PMD
    ↓
DPDK application
```

The driver directly manages:

* RX/TX descriptor rings
* packet DMA
* DPDK mbufs
* MAC configuration
* PHY/MDIO configuration
* link state
* loopback and debugging

The RP1 Ethernet device is a Linux **platform device**, so the PMD is exposed to DPDK as a `vdev` rather than through normal PCI discovery.

## Supported Hardware

Currently developed and tested for:

* Raspberry Pi 5
* RP1 I/O controller
* onboard Cadence MACB/GEM Ethernet controller
* 64-bit Linux

Other MACB/GEM devices are not currently guaranteed to work.

## Build

Install dependencies:

```bash
sudo apt update

sudo apt install -y \
    build-essential \
    meson \
    ninja-build \
    pkg-config \
    libnuma-dev \
    python3-pyelftools
```

Clone the repository:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/rachel275/dpdk-rpi.git
cd dpdk-rpi
```

Checkout the RP1 development branch:

```bash
git checkout rp1-macb-debug
```

Build:

```bash
meson setup build
ninja -C build
```

## Hugepages

Allocate hugepages:

```bash
echo 1024 | sudo tee /proc/sys/vm/nr_hugepages

sudo mkdir -p /dev/hugepages
sudo mount -t hugetlbfs nodev /dev/hugepages
```

Check:

```bash
grep Huge /proc/meminfo
```

## Bind RP1 Ethernet to UIO

> **Warning:** This removes `eth0` from the Linux networking stack.
> Do not do this over an SSH connection that uses `eth0`.

Find the RP1 Ethernet platform device:

```bash
NAME=$(basename "$(readlink /sys/class/net/eth0/device)")
echo "$NAME"
```

Bring the interface down:

```bash
sudo ip link set eth0 down
```

Unbind the Linux MACB driver:

```bash
echo "$NAME" | sudo tee \
    /sys/bus/platform/devices/$NAME/driver/unbind
```

Load UIO:

```bash
sudo modprobe uio
sudo modprobe uio_pdrv_genirq
```

Bind the device:

```bash
echo uio_pdrv_genirq | sudo tee \
    /sys/bus/platform/devices/$NAME/driver_override

echo "$NAME" | sudo tee \
    /sys/bus/platform/drivers/uio_pdrv_genirq/bind
```

Check that the device exists:

```bash
ls -l /dev/uio*
```

Typically this will create:

```text
/dev/uio0
```

## Run with `testpmd`

Example:

```bash
sudo env MACB_RXTX_TRACE=2 \
./build/app/dpdk-testpmd \
    -l 1-2 \
    -n 1 \
    --iova=pa \
    --huge-dir=/dev/hugepages \
    --vdev='net_macb0,dev=/dev/uio0,phy_mode=auto' \
    -- \
    --interactive \
    --nb-cores=1 \
    --rxd=128 \
    --txd=128
```

Inside `testpmd`:

```text
show port info all
show port stats all
start
stop
```

## PMD Options

Example:

```bash
--vdev='net_macb0,dev=/dev/uio0,phy_addr=1,phy_lb=0,mac_lb=0,phy_mode=auto'
```

Important options:

| Option     | Description                      |
| ---------- | -------------------------------- |
| `dev`      | UIO device, e.g. `/dev/uio0`     |
| `phy_addr` | PHY MDIO address                 |
| `phy_lb`   | Enable PHY loopback              |
| `mac_lb`   | Enable MAC loopback              |
| `phy_mode` | PHY speed/duplex mode            |
| `bus_ofs`  | CPU-to-device DMA address offset |

Supported PHY modes include:

```text
auto
10hd
10fd
100hd
100fd
```

## DMA Cache Synchronisation

Some RP1 DMA configurations require explicit CPU/DMA cache synchronisation.

A companion kernel helper is available here:

https://github.com/rachel275/dma-sync-helper

When used, the PMD can be pointed at the helper with:

```bash
export MACB_DMA_SYNC_NODE=/dev/dma_sync_eth0
```

## Example Application

The repository also includes a small RX test application:

```bash
meson setup build -Dexamples=matrix-rx
ninja -C build
```

Run:

```bash
sudo ./build/examples/dpdk-matrix-rx \
    --vdev=net_macb0,dev=/dev/uio0,phy_mode=auto \
    -l 0-1 \
    -n 1 \
    --no-pci
```

## Repository Structure

The main driver implementation is under:

```text
drivers/net/macb/
```

Key files include:

```text
macb_ethdev.c    Device setup, PHY and DPDK ethdev integration
macb_rxtx.c      RX/TX datapath
macb_uio.c       UIO register mapping
macb_hw.h        Hardware registers and descriptor definitions
```

For more detailed implementation and debugging notes, see:

```text
repo_walkthrough.md
matrix_test.md
```

## Development Status

The driver is still experimental.

Current development focuses on:

* reliable RX/TX
* DMA addressing
* cache coherency
* descriptor ownership
* PHY configuration
* performance optimisation

## Upstream

This repository is based on the upstream DPDK project:

https://github.com/DPDK/dpdk
